---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import DiagramFlow from '../../components/DiagramFlow.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Block storage for media infrastructure | The Atom by 33Mega"
  description="Resilient block storage for the systems around your content: virtualisation, databases and playout infrastructure on the same fully supported Ceph platform."
>
  <section class="band halftone-cyan">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">The Atom · Block</p>
      <h1>Block storage for everything around the content.</h1>
      <p class="lead" style="max-width: 820px;">
        A media facility is more than media files: virtual machines, databases, asset-management
        back ends, playout and automation systems all need storage too. The Atom serves them
        resilient block volumes from the same cluster your content lives on.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="/contact/">Talk through your infrastructure</a>
        <a class="button ghost" href="/atom/">Platform overview</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band white">
    <div class="section-inner grid-2">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Where it fits</p>
        <h2>One platform for the whole machine room.</h2>
        <p>
          Most facilities end up with a small zoo of storage: a NAS for files, an archive for
          content, and a separate SAN just to keep the VMs and databases happy. Three systems,
          three support contracts, three things to go wrong at 2am.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Atom collapses that zoo. Its block service provides resilient virtual disks for
          hypervisors and databases from the same self-healing cluster — one platform, one team,
          one SLA covering the lot.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="pop-panel violet">
        <h3>Typical block use cases</h3>
        <ul class="check-list">
          <li>Virtual machine storage for VMware, Proxmox and KVM environments.</li>
          <li>Databases behind MAM, traffic, scheduling and automation systems.</li>
          <li>Playout, ingest and channel-in-a-box infrastructure.</li>
          <li>Kubernetes persistent volumes for containerised media services.</li>
          <li>Boot and system volumes consolidated off ageing SANs.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">How it flows</p>
      <h2>Virtual disks, physically dependable.</h2>
      <DiagramFlow
        protocol="RBD / iSCSI"
        caption="Hypervisors and databases consume block volumes from The Atom; every volume is protected across the cluster and rebuilt automatically on failure."
        stages={[
          { title: 'Consumers', lines: ['Hypervisors and VMs', 'Databases and app servers', 'Kubernetes workloads'] },
          { title: 'Block service', lines: ['RBD virtual block devices', 'Snapshots and clones', 'Thin provisioning'] },
          { title: 'The Atom cluster', lines: ['Distributed across nodes', 'Replication or erasure coding', 'Self-healing, monitored 24×7'] },
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band white">
    <div class="section-inner grid-2">
      <div class="pop-panel cream">
        <h3>Fewer vendors, fewer 2am arguments</h3>
        <p>
          When the VM store, the file share and the archive are one platform, there is no
          finger-pointing between suppliers — a failure anywhere is ours to fix, end to end,
          with a four-hour software SLA and next-business-day hardware replacement behind it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Same guarantees</p>
        <h2>Infrastructure-grade, media-tested.</h2>
        <ul class="check-list">
          <li>Volumes survive drive and node failure by design.</li>
          <li>Snapshots and clones for fast recovery and test environments.</li>
          <li>Performance sized to the job with NVMe acceleration.</li>
          <li>Scales alongside your file and object capacity — no separate island.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    title="One cluster for content and infrastructure."
    text="Tell us what runs in your machine room and we'll show you what consolidating onto The Atom looks like — including what it saves."
  />
</Base>
